# LARAVEL: HOW ROUTES ARE CALLED, SENT, AND USED IN BLADE VIEWS

============================================================
1. INTRODUCTION
============================================================

In Laravel, routes connect URLs to controller methods or other actions.

Blade views use routes to:

1. Generate URLs.
2. Create navigation links.
3. Submit forms.
4. Send data to controllers.
5. Display dynamic links for database records.
6. Redirect users after operations.
7. Pass route parameters and query parameters.
8. Generate pagination links.
9. Check the currently active route.
10. Generate signed URLs.

Example project: Employee Management System.

The complete flow is:

Browser
   |
   | Requests /employees
   v
routes/web.php
   |
   | Matches the URL and HTTP method
   v
EmployeeController@index
   |
   | Fetches employees from the database
   v
return view('employees.index', ['employees' => $employees])
   |
   v
resources/views/employees/index.blade.php
   |
   | Generates links and forms
   v
Browser displays HTML
   |
   | User clicks a link or submits a form
   v
Browser sends another HTTP request
   |
   v
Laravel matches the route and executes its action

IMPORTANT:

Generating a URL does not immediately execute the route.

For example:

<a href="{{ route('employees.index') }}">Employees</a>

Laravel generates the URL. The browser sends a GET request when the user clicks the link.

============================================================
2. REGISTER ROUTES
============================================================

File: routes/web.php

<?php

use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Route;
use App\Http\Controllers\EmployeeController;

Route::get('/employees', [EmployeeController::class, 'index'])
    ->name('employees.index');

Route::get('/employees/create', [EmployeeController::class, 'create'])
    ->name('employees.create');

Route::post('/employees', [EmployeeController::class, 'store'])
    ->name('employees.store');

Route::get('/employees/{employee}', [EmployeeController::class, 'show'])
    ->name('employees.show');

Route::get('/employees/{employee}/edit', [EmployeeController::class, 'edit'])
    ->name('employees.edit');

Route::put('/employees/{employee}', [EmployeeController::class, 'update'])
    ->name('employees.update');

Route::patch('/employees/{employee}', [EmployeeController::class, 'update'])
    ->name('employees.patch');

Route::delete('/employees/{employee}', [EmployeeController::class, 'destroy'])
    ->name('employees.destroy');


UNDERSTANDING A ROUTE:

Route::get(
    '/employees/{employee}',
    [EmployeeController::class, 'show']
)->name('employees.show');

Route::get()
    Defines a route that accepts a GET request.

'/employees/{employee}'
    Defines the URL pattern.
    {employee} is a route parameter.

[EmployeeController::class, 'show']
    Specifies the controller and method to execute.

->name('employees.show')
    Assigns a name to the route.

WHY USE NAMED ROUTES?

Instead of writing the URL manually in every Blade file, use:

route('employees.index')

If the URL changes later, the named route can continue working without requiring changes to every Blade link.

============================================================
3. METHOD 1: route() HELPER
============================================================

The route() helper generates a URL using a named route.

BLADE:

<a href="{{ route('employees.index') }}">
    View All Employees
</a>

If the registered route is:

Route::get('/employees', [EmployeeController::class, 'index'])
    ->name('employees.index');

Laravel generates a URL similar to:

/employees

The complete URL depends on the application's configured URL.

HOW IT WORKS:

1. Blade evaluates the PHP expression.
2. Laravel finds the route named employees.index.
3. Laravel generates the URL.
4. Blade places the generated URL into the HTML.
5. The browser displays the link.
6. The browser sends a GET request when the user clicks it.

MORE EXAMPLES:

<a href="{{ route('employees.index') }}">Employee List</a>

<a href="{{ route('employees.create') }}">Add Employee</a>

<a href="{{ route('dashboard') }}">Dashboard</a>

Use route() when generating URLs for application routes.

The named route must exist.

============================================================
4. METHOD 2: PASS ROUTE PARAMETERS
============================================================

ROUTE:

Route::get('/employees/{employee}', [EmployeeController::class, 'show'])
    ->name('employees.show');


A. PASS THE ID DIRECTLY

<a href="{{ route('employees.show', 5) }}">
    View Employee
</a>

Generated URL:

/employees/5


B. PASS A DYNAMIC ID

@foreach ($employees as $employee)

    <p>{{ $employee->name }}</p>

    <a href="{{ route('employees.show', $employee->id) }}">
        View Employee
    </a>

@endforeach

If employee IDs are 5, 8, and 12, Laravel generates:

/employees/5
/employees/8
/employees/12


C. PASS PARAMETERS USING AN ASSOCIATIVE ARRAY

<a href="{{ route('employees.show', ['employee' => $employee->id]) }}">
    View Employee
</a>

The key employee corresponds to the route parameter {employee}.


D. PASS MULTIPLE PARAMETERS

ROUTE:

Route::get(
    '/departments/{department}/employees/{employee}',
    [EmployeeController::class, 'show']
)->name('departments.employees.show');


BLADE:

<a href="{{ route('departments.employees.show', [
    'department' => $department->id,
    'employee' => $employee->id,
]) }}">
    View Employee
</a>

Generated URL example:

/departments/2/employees/5


E. PASS AN ELOQUENT MODEL

<a href="{{ route('employees.show', $employee) }}">
    View Employee
</a>

Laravel normally uses the model's route key, which is usually its ID.

IMPORTANT:

Route parameter names must match the parameters expected by the route.

============================================================
5. METHOD 3: url() HELPER
============================================================

The url() helper generates a URL from a path.

EXAMPLE:

<a href="{{ url('/employees') }}">
    Employee List
</a>

DYNAMIC URL:

<a href="{{ url('/employees/' . $employee->id) }}">
    View Employee
</a>

For employee ID 5, the generated path is:

/employees/5


PASS A COMPLETE PATH:

<a href="{{ url('/departments/2/employees/5') }}">
    View Employee
</a>


DIFFERENCE BETWEEN route() AND url():

route('employees.index')

    Generates a URL using a named route.
    Laravel uses the registered route definition.

url('/employees')

    Generates a URL from the specified path.
    Laravel does not look up a named route.

WHY PREFER route()?

Suppose the route changes from:

/employees

to:

/staff

If you use:

<a href="{{ route('employees.index') }}">Employees</a>

and keep the route name employees.index, Laravel generates the updated URL.

But if you use:

<a href="{{ url('/employees') }}">Employees</a>

you must manually update the path.

============================================================
6. METHOD 4: action() HELPER
============================================================

The action() helper generates a URL for a controller action.

EXAMPLE:

<a href="{{ action([EmployeeController::class, 'index']) }}">
    Employee List
</a>

WITH A ROUTE PARAMETER:

<a href="{{ action(
    [EmployeeController::class, 'show'],
    ['employee' => $employee->id]
) }}">
    View Employee
</a>

Laravel must be able to find a matching route for the controller action and parameters.

For ordinary application navigation, route() is usually clearer.

IMPORTANT:

action() generates a URL.
It does not execute the controller method immediately.

