7. METHOD 5: GET THE CURRENT URL
============================================================

Laravel provides request helpers for accessing information about the current request.

CURRENT URL:

{{ request()->url() }}

FULL URL INCLUDING QUERY STRING:

{{ request()->fullUrl() }}

CURRENT PATH:

{{ request()->path() }}

HTTP METHOD:

{{ request()->method() }}


EXAMPLE REQUEST:

/employees?department_id=2

POSSIBLE RESULTS:

request()->url()
    https://example.com/employees

request()->fullUrl()
    https://example.com/employees?department_id=2

request()->path()
    employees

request()->method()
    GET


GET THE CURRENT ROUTE NAME:

{{ request()->route()?->getName() }}


CHECK THE CURRENT ROUTE:

@if (request()->routeIs('employees.index'))

    <p>You are viewing the employee list.</p>

@endif


CHECK A GROUP OF ROUTES:

@if (request()->routeIs('employees.*'))

    <p>You are inside the employee section.</p>

@endif


HIGHLIGHT THE ACTIVE NAVIGATION LINK:

<a
    href="{{ route('employees.index') }}"
    class="{{ request()->routeIs('employees.index') ? 'active' : '' }}"
>
    Employees
</a>

The active CSS class is applied when the current route is employees.index.

============================================================
8. METHOD 6: NAVIGATION LINKS USING href
============================================================

The HTML anchor tag creates a link.

EXAMPLES:

<a href="{{ route('employees.index') }}">Employee List</a>

<a href="{{ route('employees.create') }}">Add Employee</a>

<a href="{{ route('employees.show', $employee) }}">View Employee</a>

<a href="{{ route('employees.edit', $employee) }}">Edit Employee</a>

When clicked, these links normally send GET requests.

USE LINKS FOR:

1. Navigation.
2. Opening a record.
3. Opening an edit page.
4. Moving between pages.

DO NOT USE GET LINKS FOR DELETING OR MODIFYING DATABASE RECORDS.

Destructive actions should use forms with the appropriate HTTP method.

============================================================
9. METHOD 7: GET FORM
============================================================

GET forms commonly send search, filtering, sorting, and pagination parameters through the URL.

EXAMPLE: SEARCH EMPLOYEES

File: resources/views/employees/index.blade.php

<form action="{{ route('employees.index') }}" method="GET">

    <input
        type="text"
        name="search"
        placeholder="Search employees"
        value="{{ request('search') }}"
    >

    <button type="submit">Search</button>

</form>


IF THE USER SEARCHES FOR GAURAV:

/employees?search=Gaurav


CONTROLLER:

use App\Models\Employee;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;

public function index(Request $request)
{
    $search = $request->query('search');

    $employees = Employee::query()
        ->when($search, function ($query, $search) {
            $query->where(
                'name',
                'like',
                '%' . $search . '%'
            );
        })
        ->get();

    return view('employees.index', [
        'employees' => $employees,
        'search' => $search,
    ]);
}


GET A QUERY PARAMETER:

$request->query('search');

GET A GENERAL REQUEST INPUT:

$request->input('search');


The query() method specifically reads query-string parameters.

The input() method can retrieve submitted input from the request, including query-string input.

USE GET FOR:

1. Searching.
2. Filtering.
3. Sorting.
4. Pagination.
5. Report filters.

Avoid GET for creating, updating, or deleting records.

============================================================
10. METHOD 8: POST FORM
============================================================

POST is commonly used to create a database record.


STEP 1: REGISTER THE ROUTE

Route::post('/employees', [EmployeeController::class, 'store'])
    ->name('employees.store');


STEP 2: CREATE THE FORM

File: resources/views/employees/create.blade.php

<form action="{{ route('employees.store') }}" method="POST">

    @csrf

    <label for="name">Employee Name</label>

    <input
        type="text"
        id="name"
        name="name"
        value="{{ old('name') }}"
    >

    @error('name')
        <p>{{ $message }}</p>
    @enderror


    <label for="email">Email</label>

    <input
        type="email"
        id="email"
        name="email"
        value="{{ old('email') }}"
    >

    @error('email')
        <p>{{ $message }}</p>
    @enderror


    <button type="submit">Create Employee</button>

</form>


STEP 3: PROCESS THE REQUEST

public function store(Request $request)
{
    $validated = $request->validate([
        'name' => ['required', 'string', 'max:255'],
        'email' => ['required', 'email', 'unique:employees,email'],
    ]);

    $employee = Employee::create($validated);

    return to_route('employees.show', $employee)
        ->with('success', 'Employee created successfully.');
}


IMPORTANT PARTS:

action="{{ route('employees.store') }}"

    Specifies where the form submits.

method="POST"

    Specifies the HTTP method.

@csrf

    Adds the CSRF token required for a normal session-based web form.

name="name"

    Identifies the submitted field.

$request->input('name')

    Retrieves the submitted name.

$request->validate([...])

    Validates the submitted data.

Employee::create($validated)

    Creates the database record if validation passes and the model permits mass assignment.

to_route('employees.show', $employee)

    Redirects the browser to the employee's show page.


WHY REDIRECT AFTER POST?

Redirecting after a successful POST helps prevent accidental duplicate submissions when the user refreshes the resulting page.

This is commonly called the Post/Redirect/Get pattern.

============================================================
11. METHOD 9: PUT FORM
============================================================

PUT is commonly used for updating an existing record.


STEP 1: REGISTER THE ROUTE

Route::put('/employees/{employee}', [EmployeeController::class, 'update'])
    ->name('employees.update');


STEP 2: CREATE THE EDIT FORM

File: resources/views/employees/edit.blade.php

<form action="{{ route('employees.update', $employee) }}" method="POST">

    @csrf

    @method('PUT')

    <label for="name">Employee Name</label>

    <input
        type="text"
        id="name"
        name="name"
        value="{{ old('name', $employee->name) }}"
    >

    <label for="email">Email</label>

    <input
        type="email"
        id="email"
        name="email"
        value="{{ old('email', $employee->email) }}"
    >

    <button type="submit">Update Employee</button>

</form>


WHY USE @method('PUT')?

Standard HTML forms support GET and POST directly.

They do not directly support PUT, PATCH, or DELETE.

Laravel uses method spoofing.

@method('PUT')

generates a hidden input similar to:

<input type="hidden" name="_method" value="PUT">

The browser submits POST, and Laravel recognizes the _method field as PUT.


STEP 3: UPDATE THE RECORD

public function update(Request $request, Employee $employee)
{
    $validated = $request->validate([
        'name' => ['required', 'string', 'max:255'],
        'email' => [
            'required',
            'email',
            'unique:employees,email,' . $employee->id,
        ],
    ]);

    $employee->update($validated);

    return to_route('employees.show', $employee)
        ->with('success', 'Employee updated successfully.');
}


The example assumes a standard ID primary key.

Laravel's Rule::unique()->ignore($employee) is an alternative for uniqueness validation when working with model instances.

============================================================
12. METHOD 10: PATCH FORM
============================================================

PATCH is commonly used for partial updates.

ROUTE:

Route::patch('/employees/{employee}', [EmployeeController::class, 'update'])
    ->name('employees.patch');


BLADE:

<form action="{{ route('employees.patch', $employee) }}" method="POST">

    @csrf

    @method('PATCH')

    <input
        type="text"
        name="name"
        value="{{ old('name', $employee->name) }}"
    >

    <button type="submit">Update Name</button>

</form>


PUT:
    Commonly used for a complete resource update.

PATCH:
    Commonly used for a partial resource update.

The controller should validate and update only the fields permitted to change.

============================================================
13. METHOD 11: DELETE FORM
============================================================

Deleting a record should use a DELETE request, not a normal GET link.


STEP 1: REGISTER THE ROUTE

Route::delete('/employees/{employee}', [EmployeeController::class, 'destroy'])
    ->name('employees.destroy');


STEP 2: CREATE THE FORM

<form
    action="{{ route('employees.destroy', $employee) }}"
    method="POST"
    onsubmit="return confirm('Are you sure you want to delete this employee?')"
>

    @csrf

    @method('DELETE')

    <button type="submit">Delete Employee</button>

</form>


STEP 3: CONTROLLER

public function destroy(Employee $employee)
{
    $employee->delete();

    return to_route('employees.index')
        ->with('success', 'Employee deleted successfully.');
}


FLOW:

User clicks Delete
    |
    v
Browser submits POST
    |
    | Contains CSRF token and _method=DELETE
    v
Laravel recognizes DELETE
    |
    v
employees.destroy route
    |
    v
EmployeeController@destroy
    |
    v
Employee is deleted
    |
    v
Browser is redirected to employees.index


IMPORTANT:

The JavaScript confirmation dialog is not security.

Always enforce authorization on the server before deleting a record.

