20. METHOD 18: ROUTE-MODEL BINDING
============================================================

ROUTE:

Route::get('/employees/{employee}', [EmployeeController::class, 'show'])
    ->name('employees.show');


CONTROLLER:

public function show(Employee $employee)
{
    return view('employees.show', [
        'employee' => $employee,
    ]);
}


BLADE:

<a href="{{ route('employees.show', $employee) }}">
    View Employee
</a>


Laravel uses the route parameter and the Employee type hint to retrieve the corresponding model through implicit route-model binding.

If the record does not exist, Laravel normally returns a 404 response.


CUSTOM ROUTE KEY EXAMPLE:

Route::get('/employees/{employee:email}', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'show',
])->name('employees.show');


This tells Laravel to bind the employee using the email column rather than the default route key.

Be careful about exposing personal information, such as email addresses, in URLs.

