22. METHOD 20: SIGNED ROUTES
============================================================

Signed URLs help detect URL tampering.

They are useful for certain invitations, verification flows, and temporary links.


REGISTER THE ROUTE:

Route::get('/employee-invitation/{employee}', [
    EmployeeInvitationController::class,
    'show',
])
    ->name('employees.invitation')
    ->middleware('signed');


GENERATE A TEMPORARY SIGNED URL:

use Illuminate\Support\Facades\URL;

$url = URL::temporarySignedRoute(
    'employees.invitation',
    now()->addMinutes(30),
    ['employee' => $employee->id]
);


PASS THE URL TO A VIEW:

return view('employees.invitation-email', [
    'invitationUrl' => $url,
]);


BLADE:

<a href="{{ $invitationUrl }}">Open Invitation</a>


The signed middleware validates the signature and expiration.

A signed URL does not replace authentication or authorization when those are required.

