23. METHOD 21: PAGINATION LINKS
============================================================

CONTROLLER:

public function index(Request $request)
{
    $employees = Employee::paginate(10);

    return view('employees.index', [
        'employees' => $employees,
    ]);
}


BLADE:

@foreach ($employees as $employee)

    <p>{{ $employee->name }}</p>

@endforeach

{{ $employees->links() }}


Laravel generates pagination links using query parameters such as:

/employees?page=1
/employees?page=2
/employees?page=3


PRESERVE QUERY PARAMETERS:

$employees = Employee::query()
    ->when($request->query('search'), function ($query, $search) {
        $query->where('name', 'like', '%' . $search . '%');
    })
    ->paginate(10)
    ->withQueryString();


withQueryString() preserves the existing query parameters when generating pagination links.

For example:

/employees?search=Gaurav&page=2

============================================================
24. METHOD 22: REDIRECTING VERSUS GENERATING URLS
============================================================

GENERATE A URL:

$url = route('employees.index');


CREATE A BLADE LINK:

<a href="{{ route('employees.index') }}">Employees</a>


REDIRECT FROM A CONTROLLER:

return to_route('employees.index');


REDIRECT A PATH AT THE ROUTING LEVEL:

Route::redirect('/staff', '/employees');


These operations have different purposes.

A URL helper generates a URL.

An anchor tag lets the browser navigate to a URL.

A redirect response instructs the browser to navigate elsewhere.

A redirect route sends requests from one path to another.

============================================================
25. METHOD 23: URL FACADE
============================================================

The URL facade can also generate URLs for named routes.

BLADE:

{{ \Illuminate\Support\Facades\URL::route('employees.index') }}


PHP:

use Illuminate\Support\Facades\URL;

$url = URL::route('employees.show', [
    'employee' => $employee->id,
]);


For ordinary Blade templates, route() is usually shorter and easier to read.

============================================================
26. METHOD 24: href, action, AND route()
============================================================

A. href

<a href="{{ route('employees.index') }}">Employees</a>

href is an HTML attribute specifying the destination of a link.


B. action

<form action="{{ route('employees.store') }}" method="POST">

    @csrf

    <input name="name">

    <button type="submit">Save</button>

</form>

action is an HTML attribute specifying the destination of a form submission.


C. route()

{{ route('employees.index') }}

route() is a Laravel helper that generates a URL using a named route.


SUMMARY:

href
    Used for HTML links.

action
    Used for HTML forms.

route()
    Generates a URL.

None of these operations, by itself, immediately executes a controller action.

